TP.register({id:"lp5-g4",lp:5,grade:"4",mode:"curriculum",checks:4,nextGrade:"5th grade",build(E){const F=E.F;const FONT=`font-family="Andika,sans-serif" font-weight="700"`;const SLED="The radio crackled at six in the morning, and Tova was already dressed. Her boots stood by the tent door, stuffed with dry socks so they would not freeze stiff.\n\nHer job was to ride out with the dog team and read the weather station at the far end of the bay. Nine huskies waited in their harnesses, breathing little clouds. They did not bark. They leaned.\n\n“Hold the handle with both hands,” said Ravi, the driver. “If you fall off, let go and stay still. I will come back for you.”\n\nThe sled slid forward, then flew. Flags marked the route every hundred meters, red cloth snapping in the wind, and Tova counted them out loud to keep her mind off the cold. At flag twenty-two the station appeared, a small white box on tall legs.\n\nThe thermometer read thirty-one degrees below zero. Tova wrote the number down twice, because her hand was shaking and she wanted the log to be right.";const TENT="Jonah drew the midnight shift, which meant two hours of sitting up in the cook tent while the rest of the team slept. His one job was simple. Keep the stove going and listen to the radio.\n\nAt ten past one, the stove flame shrank to a thin blue line. Jonah checked the fuel can. Empty. The spare cans were stacked outside under a tarp, forty steps away, and the wind was loud enough that he could not hear the radio anymore.\n\nHe wrote the time in the log, clipped his headlamp to his hat, and tied a rope from the tent pole to his belt before he opened the flap. In blowing snow, forty steps can turn into a lost person. He came back with two cans and a face full of snow, and the flame climbed again.";const FOX="Nobody believed Priya about the fox until the second morning.\n\nShe had found the tracks first, a neat line of small prints that came out of the snow near flag nine, circled the food crate, and went back the way they came. “A bird,” said Ravi, without looking up from the stove.\n\nSo Priya set her camera on a crate and pointed it at the flag line. She checked it after breakfast. In the first picture the snow was empty. In the second there was a white shape with black eyes, standing still as a stone. In the third the shape was gone and the snow was empty again.\n\nRavi looked at the pictures for a long time. “Flag nine,” he said. “Tomorrow I am going with you.”";const CORE="Snow that falls on a polar ice sheet does not melt. New snow presses down on old snow, year after year, until the bottom layers turn to solid ice. Scientists drill a long round stick of that ice out of the ground and call it an ice core. A core is a record of past weather. Summer snow and winter snow pack differently, so a core has bands, the way a tree has rings. Counting the bands tells how many years of snow are in the core. A gray band means a volcano put ash in the sky that year. A thin band means a dry year with little snowfall. Air is trapped in the ice too. Tiny bubbles hold samples of the air from the year the snow fell, so a team can test air that is thousands of years old.";const PEN_A={title:"The Huddle",body:"Emperor penguins raise their chicks in the coldest months of the year, out on sea ice far from open water. There are no trees and no shelter, so the birds build their own. Thousands of them crowd together into a huddle, packed so tightly that a person could not walk between them. The air inside a huddle can be more than 20 degrees warmer than the air outside. Birds on the windy edge shuffle along the side and tuck into the back, and the whole huddle drifts a little as they trade places. No bird stays out in the wind for long."};const PEN_B={title:"Built for Cold",body:"An emperor penguin has about 100 feathers on every square inch of skin. The feathers overlap like shingles on a roof and lock out wind and water. Under the skin is a layer of fat as thick as a book. A penguin loses heat fast through its feet and flippers, so blood heading out to them runs right beside blood coming back, and the cold blood is warmed before it reaches the body again. Before the coldest months, a penguin eats until almost half its weight is fat."};const PEN_C={title:"Minding the Egg",body:"A female emperor penguin lays one egg and then walks back to the sea to feed. The male holds the egg on his feet under a flap of warm skin and eats nothing for about two months. When the female returns she brings food in her stomach for the chick, and the male starts the long walk to the sea for his first meal in weeks."};const FOX_A={title:"A Coat for Two Seasons",body:"An arctic fox is about the size of a house cat, and it does not sleep through the winter. In summer its fur is brown and gray, which hides it against rock and dirt. In fall the brown fur drops out and thick white fur grows in, so the fox is hard to see against snow. The winter coat is so warm that a fox does not start to shiver until the air drops near 40 degrees below zero. Its ears and nose are small and round, which gives the cold less skin to pull heat from."};const FOX_B={title:"Hunting the Snow",body:"Most of a fox's winter food lives under the snow. Lemmings are small, round animals that tunnel between the snow and the ground, where a fox cannot see them. So the fox listens. It turns its head until the sound is right, then jumps high and punches straight down through the crust with its front feet. Many jumps catch nothing. In good months a fox buries extra food under rocks and digs it up again when hunting is poor."};const WH_A={title:"Narwhals and Belugas",kind:"field guide",body:"Narwhals and belugas are whales that spend the whole year in the far north. Neither one has a back fin, so it can swim just under a sheet of ice without scraping it. Both whales must come up for air, so they look for a crack or a hole in the ice. A group of whales will use the same breathing hole for weeks. If new ice seals that hole shut and the next one is too far away, the group is in danger."};const WH_B={title:"Listening for Whales",kind:"website",body:"Researchers can study whales they never see. They lower a microphone built for water, called a hydrophone, through a hole in the ice and leave it there for months. Belugas squeak, whistle, and click almost without stopping, and a narwhal makes a clicking sound it uses to find its way. Each sound has a shape a computer can match, so one recording can tell the team which whales passed by and when."};const WORDS=["unloading","overnight","mislabeled","immovable","telescope","barometer","reappeared","helplessness","supervisor","unbelievable","unflemmable","disgrantion"];const SUN="Near the poles the sun does not rise and set the way it does at home. The earth is tilted, so for part of the year the top of the world leans toward the sun and the sun never drops below the horizon at all. That is the midnight sun, and a team can work at two in the morning in full daylight. For the other part of the year the same tilt leans that half of the world away, and the sun never climbs above the horizon. The sky still turns a dim blue around noon, but the sun itself stays down. Camps that far north run on clocks, not on daylight.";const ATLAS="Ravi calls the biggest husky on the team Atlas, after the giant in the old Greek stories who held up the whole sky on his shoulders. Atlas runs closest to the sled, where the pull is hardest, and Ravi says he has never once seen the dog slack his line.";function shapeSortFig(){const P=(pts,alt,o)=>F.poly(pts,Object.assign({size:160,alt},o||{}));return F.row([P([[10,20],[80,90],[10,90]],"A triangle with a square corner at the bottom left",{rightAngles:[[10,90,1,-1]]}),"A"],[P([[20,20],[90,20],[75,85],[5,85]],"A four-sided shape with both pairs of opposite sides parallel"),"B"],[P([[25,20],[75,20],[95,85],[5,85]],"A four-sided shape with one pair of parallel sides"),"C"],[P([[50,15],[92,88],[14,88]],"A triangle with no square corner"),"D"])}function paras(text,cls){return`<div class="passage${cls?" "+cls:""}">${text.split(/\n\s*\n/).map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join("")}</div>`}function oneText(title,body){return`<h3 style="margin:10px 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${title}</h3><p class="passage sm" style="max-width:none">${body}</p>`}function twoTexts(a,b,n0){const k=n0||0;return`<div class="texts"><article><h3>Text ${k+1}: ${a.title}</h3><p>${a.body}</p></article><article><h3>Text ${k+2}: ${b.title}</h3><p>${b.body}</p></article></div>`}function sourceBox(){return`<div class="texts"><article><h3>Source 1 (${WH_A.kind}): ${WH_A.title}</h3><p>${WH_A.body}</p></article><article><h3>Source 2 (${WH_B.kind}): ${WH_B.title}</h3><p>${WH_B.body}</p></article></div>`}function lookBack(label,inner,cls){return`<details class="look-back ${cls||""}" open><summary>${label}</summary>${inner}</details>`}const bullets=(...x)=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${x.map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const WRITE_DIR="Write on your paper. When you finish, hold it up or read it to your teacher.";function iceCore(){const bands=[["var(--sea-soft)","",34],["var(--surface)","",32],["var(--muted)","gray ash band",30],["var(--sea-soft)","",33],["var(--surface)","thin band",13],["var(--sea-soft)","",31],["var(--surface)","",33]];let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 560 250" width="560" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="An ice core drawn as a tall stick of ice cut into seven layers. The third layer is gray and labeled gray ash band. The fifth layer is much thinner than the others and labeled thin band.">`;let y=14;bands.forEach(b=>{s+=`<rect x="190" y="${y}" width="124" height="${b[2]}" fill="${b[0]}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/>`;if(b[1])s+=`<line x1="314" y1="${y+b[2]/2}" x2="340" y2="${y+b[2]/2}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="348" y="${y+b[2]/2}" font-size="21" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" dominant-baseline="central">${b[1]}</text>`;y+=b[2]});s+=`<text x="178" y="30" font-size="21" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="end">top: new snow</text>`;s+=`<text x="178" y="${y-8}" font-size="21" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="end">bottom: old ice</text>`;return s+"</svg>"}function barrel(x,y,w,h,frac,label,note){const ih=h-10,wh=ih*frac;return`<rect x="${x}" y="${y}" width="${w}" height="${h}" rx="10" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="4"/><rect x="${x+5}" y="${y+5+ih-wh}" width="${w-10}" height="${wh}" rx="4" fill="var(--sea)"/><text x="${x+w/2}" y="${y+h+30}" font-size="22" ${FONT} fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="middle">${label}</text><text x="${x+w/2}" y="${y+h+56}" font-size="22" ${FONT} fill="var(--gold-ink)" text-anchor="middle">${note}</text>`}function twoBarrels(){return`<svg viewBox="0 0 470 282" width="470" style="max-width:100%;height:auto" role="img" aria-label="Camp A has a big wide barrel that is one fourth full of water. Camp B has a much smaller barrel that is one half full of water.">${barrel(60,14,140,190,.25,"Camp A barrel","1/4 full")}${barrel(300,112,86,92,.5,"Camp B barrel","1/2 full")}</svg>`}const LOG_TABLE=()=>F.table(["Log entry","Number"],[["Kilometers since the ship left port","38,471"],["Kilometers sailed this season","2,650"],["Meters of sled track cut today","3,489"]],{caption:"Expedition Log"});const CAMP_TABLE=()=>F.table(["At base camp","Why"],[["Every tent has two layers","Still air between the layers holds heat in"],["The stove melts snow","It is the only drinking water for 300 kilometers"],["Ropes run from tent to tent","In blowing snow you cannot see the next tent"],["Partners check each other's faces","Frostbite starts where you cannot feel it"],["A radio call goes out every six hours","So the base ship knows the team is safe"],["Boots are dried every night","Wet boots freeze stiff and give frostbite"]],{caption:"Base Camp Rules"});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"Crate Word Flip",short:"Crate Words",parts:[{std:["RF.4.3"],kind:"words",words:WORDS,n:12,dur:90,dir:"Read each word out loud. The last two are made-up words. Just sound them out.",desc:"reading long words with prefixes, roots, and suffixes",key:["Read each word in order. Count a word correct when the student reads it right the first time or fixes it without help.","unflemmable and disgrantion are made-up words. Listen for the chunks: un-flem-ma-ble and dis-grant-ion.","Watch for prefixes (un-, mis-, im-, re-, over-, super-, dis-), roots (tele and scope, baro and meter, vis), suffixes (-ing, -ed, -able, -ness, -or, -ion), and whether the student breaks a long word into syllables or guesses from the first letters."]}]},{id:2,title:"Depot Count",short:"Ten Times",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.1"],dur:130,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain how you know.",desc:"how a digit is worth 10 times what the same digit is worth one place to its right",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A depot is a pile of supplies the team leaves out on the ice to pick up later. Every depot has a count board.</p>`,fig:()=>`<p class="huge">9,900</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">ration bars at the first depot</p>`,qs:["In 9,900, how does the 9 in the thousands place compare to the 9 in the hundreds place?","A 4 in the hundreds place is worth 400. In which place would a 4 be worth 10 times as much. What would it be worth there?","Finish this: 70,000 is ____ times as much as 7,000.","Use place value to explain why 500 ÷ 50 = 10.","The team counted 2,220 flags. Compare the 2 in the hundreds place to the 2 in the tens place. Then compare the 2 in the thousands place to the 2 in the hundreds place."],key:["It is worth 10 times as much. The 9 in the thousands place is 9,000 and the 9 in the hundreds place is 900, and 9,000 is 10 times 900. Partial: naming the two values with no comparison is approaching.","The thousands place, where a 4 would be worth 4,000. 4,000 is 10 times 400. Partial: naming the place with no value given is approaching.","10. Partial: 100 counts two places instead of one and does not count.","The 5 in the hundreds place is worth 10 times the 5 in the tens place, so 500 is 10 groups of 50. That is why the quotient is 10. Partial: working the division out with no place value reason is approaching.","200 is 10 times 20, and 2,000 is 10 times 200. Each 2 is worth 10 times the 2 one place to its right. Partial: one of the two comparisons given is approaching."]}]},{id:3,title:"Dogsled Run",short:"Dogsled",parts:[{title:"Read the Run",std:["RF.4.4"],kind:"read",n:163,label:"words read correctly",dur:105,dir:"Read the story out loud. Use your best reading voice.",desc:"a 163-word grade-level story read aloud",html:()=>paras(SLED,"g4-flu"),key:["163 words. Time 1 minute and mark errors and self-corrections on your own copy.","Midyear grade 4 benchmark: about 120 words correct per minute (50th percentile, Hasbrouck and Tindal 2017 winter norms).","Note pace and expression, such as a change of voice for what Ravi says."]},{title:"Prove It",std:["RL.4.1"],dur:160,dir:"Answer out loud. Look back at the story and point to the details that prove your answer.",desc:"questions about the dogsled story answered with details and examples from the text",html:()=>lookBack("Look back at the story",paras(SLED,"sm"),"g4-story"),after:view=>{const d=view.querySelector("details.g4-story");if(d&&view.querySelector(".g4-flu"))d.open=false},qs:["Why were Tova's boots stuffed with dry socks? Use a detail from the story.","How do you know the dogs were eager to run? Give two details from the story.","Why did Tova count the flags out loud? Use the story.","Why did Tova write the temperature down twice? Give details from the story."],key:["So the boots would not freeze stiff. The story says they stood by the tent door, stuffed with dry socks for that reason. (explicit)","They waited in their harnesses breathing little clouds, they did not bark, and they leaned, which means they were already pulling forward. (inference)","To keep her mind off the cold. The count also told her how far she had gone, because the station showed up at flag twenty-two. (explicit)","Her hand was shaking from the cold, and she wanted the log to be right, so one shaky number was not good enough. (explicit plus inference)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Round the Log",short:"Round It",parts:[{std:["4.NBT.A.3"],dur:110,dir:"Say each answer out loud.",desc:"rounding multi-digit whole numbers to a given place",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The radio operator reports round numbers, not exact ones, so the base ship can write them down fast.</p>`,fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${LOG_TABLE()}</div>`,qs:["Round 38,471 to the nearest thousand.","Round 38,471 to the nearest hundred.","Round 38,471 to the nearest ten thousand.","Round 2,650 kilometers to the nearest hundred kilometers.","Round 3,489 meters to the nearest ten meters."],key:["38,000. Partial: 39,000 rounds up when the hundreds digit is 4 and does not count.","38,500. Partial: 38,400 rounds down when the tens digit is 7 and does not count.","40,000. Partial: 30,000 rounds down when the thousands digit is 8 and does not count.","2,700 kilometers. Partial: 2,600 rounds down when the tens digit is 5 and does not count.","3,490 meters. Partial: 3,480 rounds down when the ones digit is 9 and does not count."]}]},{id:5,title:"Reading the Ice",short:"Ice Core",parts:[{std:["RI.4.1"],dur:100,dir:"Read the text to yourself. Then answer out loud using the text.",desc:"questions answered with details and examples from an informational text",html:()=>oneText("Reading the Ice",CORE),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${iceCore()}</div>`,qs:["What is an ice core? Use details from the text.","A team finds a gray band in a core. What does that tell them about that year? Use the text.","Two cores are the same length. Core A has 200 bands and Core B has 60 bands. Which place got more snow each year? Explain using the text."],key:["A long round stick of ice drilled out of an ice sheet. The text explains that new snow presses down on old snow year after year until the bottom layers become solid ice. (explicit)","A volcano put ash in the sky that year. The text gives that as what a gray band means. (explicit)","Core B. Both cores are the same length, but Core B holds only 60 years instead of 200, so each band in Core B is much thicker. The text says a thin band means a dry year with little snowfall, so thick bands mean more snow fell each year. (inference)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Two Night Stories",short:"Two Stories",parts:[{std:["RL.4.1"],n:7,dur:275,dir:"Read both stories to yourself. Then answer out loud and point to the details that prove your answer.",desc:"questions about two stories answered with details and examples from each text",html:()=>`<h3>Story 1: The Midnight Shift</h3>${paras(TENT,"sm")}<h3>Story 2: Tracks by Flag Nine</h3>${paras(FOX,"sm")}`,qs:["Story 1. What were Jonah's two jobs on the midnight shift? Use the story.","Story 1. Why did Jonah tie a rope from the tent pole to his belt? Give details from the story.","Story 1. How do you know the stove was running out of fuel before Jonah ever checked the can?","Story 1. Did Jonah do his job well? Give two examples from the story.","Story 2. How did Priya prove that a fox was coming to camp? Use details from the story.","Story 2. How do you know Ravi did not believe her at first? Give an example from the story.","Story 2. What does Ravi's last line show about how he changed? Use the story."],key:["Two jobs: keeping the stove going, and listening to the radio. The story names both. (explicit)","So he could find his way back to the tent. The story says the spare cans were forty steps away and that in blowing snow forty steps can turn into a lost person. (inference)","The flame shrank to a thin blue line at ten past one, before he picked up the can and found it empty. (inference)","Yes. He wrote the time in the log, he put on a headlamp and tied the rope before going out, and he came back with two cans so the flame climbed again. (inference)","She set her camera on a crate pointed at the flag line, and the second picture showed a white shape with black eyes standing still as a stone where the snow had been empty. (explicit)","He said “A bird,” without looking up from the stove, so he did not even stop to look at the tracks. (inference from an explicit detail)","He believes her now and wants to see it himself. He looked at the pictures for a long time, named flag nine, and said he is going with her tomorrow. (inference)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Fuel Drum Compare",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.2"],dur:190,dir:"Answer out loud and tell how you know. Write numbers 2 and 5 on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"comparing fractions with different numerators and denominators",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Fuel runs the camp stove. The team writes down how full each drum is as a fraction.</p>`,qs:["Which is greater: 2/3 of a drum or 5/8 of a drum? How do you know?","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 3/4 ____ 9/12","One drum is 3/10 full and another is 1/4 full. Which drum holds more fuel? How do you know?","Is 5/12 of a drum more or less than half a drum? Explain using half as your benchmark.","Write &gt;, &lt;, or = to make it true: 7/8 ____ 4/5","Look at the two barrels. Camp B says it has more water because 1/2 is more than 1/4. Is Camp B right? Explain."],fig2:()=>`<div class="figure">${twoBarrels()}</div>`,key:["2/3. Using twelfths does not work here, so use twenty-fourths: 2/3 is 16/24 and 5/8 is 15/24. Or: 2/3 is 1/3 from a whole and 5/8 is 3/8 from a whole, and 1/3 is the smaller gap. Partial: naming 2/3 with no common denominator and no benchmark is approaching.","3/4 = 9/12. Multiplying the top and bottom of 3/4 by 3 gives 9/12. Partial: the equals sign right with no reason is approaching.","3/10. In twentieths, 3/10 is 6/20 and 1/4 is 5/20. Partial: naming 3/10 with no common denominator shown is approaching.","Less than half. Half of 12 is 6, so 6/12 is half a drum, and 5/12 is less than 6/12. Partial: saying less with no use of 6/12 is approaching.","7/8 &gt; 4/5. In fortieths, 7/8 is 35/40 and 4/5 is 32/40. Accept also that 7/8 is 1/8 from a whole and 4/5 is 1/5 from a whole, and 1/8 is a smaller piece. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching.","No. The barrels are not the same size, so the fractions alone cannot be compared. The picture shows Camp A's barrel is much bigger, so 1/4 of it is actually more water than 1/2 of Camp B's small barrel. Fractions can be compared only when they refer to the same whole. Partial: saying no with nothing said about the wholes being the same size is approaching."]}]},{id:8,title:"Four Animal Texts",short:"Four Texts",parts:[{std:["RI.4.9"],n:4,dur:300,dir:"Read all four texts to yourself. Then answer out loud. Use facts from both texts of a pair in each answer.",desc:"putting together information from two texts on the same topic, over two different topics",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Emperor penguins live near the South Pole. Texts 1 and 2 are both about that bird.</p>${twoTexts(PEN_A,PEN_B)}<p class="big">An arctic fox lives in the far north. Texts 3 and 4 are both about that animal.</p>${twoTexts(FOX_A,FOX_B,2)}`,qs:["Use Texts 1 and 2. Explain how an emperor penguin lives through the coldest months of the year.","A penguin's feathers and fat already hold heat in very well. Use Texts 1 and 2 to explain why standing in a huddle is still worth doing.","Use Texts 3 and 4. Explain how an arctic fox gets through a polar winter.","Texts 3 and 4 both talk about being hard to see. Use both of them to explain how that works for the fox and against the fox."],key:["From Text 2: about 100 feathers per square inch that overlap and lock out wind and water, a fat layer as thick as a book, blood to the feet and flippers warmed on the way back, and eating until almost half its weight is fat. From Text 1: thousands huddle so tightly that the inside air is more than 20 degrees warmer, and they trade places so no bird stays on the windy edge. Credit an answer that uses at least one fact from each text.","Text 2 says a penguin still loses heat fast through its feet and flippers and that it has only the fat it stored to live on. Text 1 says the huddle is more than 20 degrees warmer inside and that the shuffle keeps every bird out of the wind, so the huddle saves the fat the bird needs to last all season.","From Text 3: it stays awake all winter, grows thick white fur that hides it on snow and keeps it from shivering until near 40 below, and has small round ears and a small nose that lose less heat. From Text 4: it finds food under the snow by listening and punching down through the crust, and it buries extra food under rocks for poor months. Credit an answer that uses at least one fact from each of the two fox texts.","Text 3 says its white winter fur makes the fox hard to see against snow, which helps it. Text 4 says the lemmings it hunts are also out of sight under the snow, so the fox has to hunt by sound and many jumps catch nothing, which works against it."]}]},{id:9,title:"Radio Vote",short:"Vote",parts:[{std:["W.4.1"],n:3,label:"checks met",dur:130,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short opinion piece with a reason and a closing sentence",html:()=>`<p class="big">One hour of daylight is left. The team can do one job today: dig out the supply crates buried by last night's storm, or ride to the weather station for one more reading. Which should the team do? Write 3 sentences.</p>${bullets("State your opinion","Give one reason, with a fact or detail","End with a closing sentence")}<p class="small" style="margin-top:14px">Facts you may use: the buried crates hold three days of food. A station reading is due every day, and a missed day leaves a hole in the record. Another storm is forecast for tomorrow.</p>`,key:["States an opinion about which job the team should do today","Gives a reason supported by a fact or detail, such as the three days of food, the daily reading, or tomorrow's storm","Ends with a closing sentence that matches the opinion"]}]},{id:11,title:"Whale Watch Notes",short:"Notes",parts:[{std:["W.4.8"],dur:110,dir:"Read the two sources. Write your notes on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"taking notes in their own words, sorting the notes, and listing sources",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">You are gathering facts for a report on whales that stay in the far north all year.</p>${sourceBox()}`,qs:["Write three notes. Use both sources. Keep them short and use your own words.","Sort your notes. Write a heading above each note: Living Under the Ice or How Researchers Listen.","List your sources. Next to each note, write the title of the source it came from."],key:["Three short notes that use both sources, in the student's own words and not copied sentences. Examples: no back fin so they can swim under ice; they need a crack or hole to breathe; a hydrophone is left under the ice for months; a computer matches each sound. (gathers information, takes notes, paraphrases)","Each note sits under the heading that fits. Facts about fins, breathing holes, and danger go under Living Under the Ice. Facts about hydrophones, squeaks and clicks, and matching sounds by computer go under How Researchers Listen. (categorizes)","Writes both titles, each next to the right notes: Narwhals and Belugas, and Listening for Whales. (lists sources)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Fix the Field Log",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.4.1"],dur:85,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the correct way.",desc:"spoken grammar items (relative pronouns, progressive tense, modal words, order of adjectives, run-ons, confused words)",qs:["The scientist ____ fixed the radio works at the weather station. (who or which)","Right now the dogs ____ in the snow. (rest) Start with are.","Pick the better word: The ice here is only three centimeters thick. We ____ walk on it. (must not or might not)","Fix the order: a canvas heavy green tent","Fix this run-on: The wind rose we tied down the tent.","The team left ____ skis by the flag. ____ going back for them. (their, there, or they're)"],key:["who (relative pronoun for a person)","are resting (progressive tense)","must not. Three centimeters of ice is not safe, so it is not allowed, not just possible. (modal auxiliary)","a heavy green canvas tent (order of adjectives: size, then color, then material)","Any correct fix, such as: The wind rose, so we tied down the tent. Or two sentences: The wind rose. We tied down the tent. (correcting a run-on)","their skis, and They're going back for them. (frequently confused words)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Base Camp Writer",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"facts",label:"Facts",icon:"📋",hint:"Explain how the camp stays safe"},std:["W.4.2"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an informative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">How does the team stay warm and safe at base camp? Write to explain it. Use facts from the chart. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${bullets("Introduce your topic","Give facts, details, or examples","Use linking words: for example, also, because","End with a concluding sentence")}<div class="figure">${CAMP_TABLE()}</div>`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly","Develops the topic with facts and details from the chart, such as two tent layers holding still air, melting snow for water, ropes between tents, face checks for frostbite, radio calls every six hours, or drying boots","Links ideas with words like for example, also, because, another","Ends with a concluding statement"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"opinion",label:"Opinion",icon:"💬",hint:"Dogsled or snowmobile?"},std:["W.4.1"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:270,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"an opinion writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">Should the expedition travel by dogsled or by snowmobile? Write your opinion. Use facts from the chart. Write 5 to 7 sentences.</p>${bullets("State your opinion","Give reasons with facts or details","Use linking words: for instance, in addition, in order to","End with a concluding sentence")}<div class="figure">${F.table(["Dogsled","Snowmobile"],[["Travels about 10 km in an hour","Travels about 30 km in an hour"],["Each dog eats about 1 kg of food a day","Needs fuel, carried on a second sled"],["Dogs often stop at thin ice on their own","A driver has to spot thin ice"],["Nine dogs and a sled, no engine to fix","Can stop running in very deep cold"]],{caption:"Two Ways to Travel"})}</div>`,key:["Introduces the topic and states an opinion","Gives reasons supported by facts and details from the chart, such as speed, food or fuel, thin ice, or breaking down in deep cold","Links the opinion and the reasons with words like for instance, in addition, in order to","Ends with a concluding statement"]}]},{id:15,title:"What the Words Mean",short:"Word Meaning",parts:[{std:["RL.4.4"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Read the short piece. Then answer out loud.",desc:"explaining what words and phrases mean as they are used in a text, including a name that alludes to a character from an old story",html:()=>`${paras(ATLAS,"sm")}<p class="big" style="margin-top:6px">Two more lines from the dogsled story:</p>${bullets("The sled slid forward, then flew.","They did not bark. They leaned.")}`,qs:["Why is Atlas a good name for that dog? Use what the piece tells you about the giant in the old Greek stories.","The story says the sled flew. The sled has no wings, so what does flew mean here?","What does leaned tell you about the dogs. How is that different from saying they stood?"],key:["In the old stories Atlas carried the heaviest load there was, the whole sky. This dog runs where the pull is hardest and never slacks his line, so he carries the heaviest share of the work. (RL.4.4, allusion)","It means the sled went very fast. The writer uses flew to show speed, not that the sled left the ground. (RL.4.4)","Leaned means the dogs were already pulling forward into their harnesses, eager to go. Stood would mean they were just waiting, doing nothing. (RL.4.4)"]}]},{id:16,title:"The Midnight Sun",short:"Listen",parts:[{std:["SL.4.2"],n:3,dur:60,read:SUN,dir:"Put your pencil down and listen. Then say each answer in your own words.",desc:"paraphrasing portions of a text read aloud",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="text-align:center;margin-top:0">Listening time. Your teacher will read a short report about daylight at the poles.</p><p class="small" style="text-align:center">The horizon is the line where the sky seems to meet the ground.</p>`,key:["Ask: Put the first part in your own words. Why does the sun stay up all day and all night for part of the year? (Because the earth is tilted, and for part of the year that half of the world leans toward the sun, so the sun never goes below the horizon. Count it correct only when the student uses their own words rather than repeating the sentence back.) (SL.4.2)","Ask: Put the middle part in your own words. What happens for the other part of the year? (The same tilt leans that half away from the sun, so the sun never comes up at all.) (SL.4.2)","Ask: Put the last two sentences in your own words. (Even when the sun never rises, the sky still goes dim blue in the middle of the day, and the camps follow their clocks instead of the daylight.) (SL.4.2)","Read the report once at a steady pace. If you read it a second time, note that you did."]}]},{id:17,title:"Fox Report",short:"Report Out",parts:[{std:["SL.4.4"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:55,dir:"Give a one-minute report out loud. Speak clearly and not too fast.",desc:"reporting on a topic in an organized way with facts and descriptive details",html:()=>`<p class="big">Give a short report on the arctic fox for someone who has never heard of one. Use the two texts.</p>${bullets("Start by saying what your report is about","Group your facts: first how the fox stays warm, then how it finds food","Add details that help the listener picture it","Speak clearly, loud enough, and not too fast")}${lookBack("Look back at the two fox texts",twoTexts(FOX_A,FOX_B),"g4-fox")}`,key:["Organized: opens by naming the topic, groups the facts rather than jumping about, and finishes (SL.4.4)","Uses appropriate facts from the texts, such as the coat that turns white in fall, small round ears and nose, no shivering until near 40 below, listening for lemmings under the snow, jumping and punching through the crust, burying extra food (SL.4.4)","Adds relevant descriptive details that support the main points, such as about the size of a house cat, or many jumps catch nothing (SL.4.4)","Speaks clearly at an understandable pace and at a volume you can hear (SL.4.4)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Times As Many",short:"Compare",parts:[{std:["4.OA.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Write the equations on your paper and hold them up.",desc:"reading a multiplication equation as a comparison and writing a comparison as an equation",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">A dogsled travels about 10 kilometers in an hour. A snowmobile travels about 30 kilometers in an hour.</p>`,qs:["Write a multiplication equation that compares the two speeds, and say it in words.","Read 35 = 5 × 7 as a comparison. Say it two different ways.","The depot holds 4 times as many ration bars as the camp. The camp has 120 bars. Write an equation with a letter for the unknown, then find the number."],key:["30 = 3 × 10. In words: the snowmobile goes 3 times as fast as the dogsled. 10 × 3 = 30 is the same equation. Partial: the equation right with no words given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","35 is 5 times as many as 7, and 35 is 7 times as many as 5. Partial: one of the two ways given is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)","b = 4 × 120, so b = 480 ration bars. Partial: the equation right with no number found is approaching. (4.OA.A.1)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Same Amount of Fuel",short:"Equal Parts",parts:[{std:["4.NF.A.1"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Look at the two drums. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining and generating equivalent fractions with a visual model",fig:()=>F.row([F.fracBar(4,3,{width:300}),"Drum A, marked in 4 equal parts"],[F.fracBar(8,6,{width:300}),"Drum B, marked in 8 equal parts"]),qs:["What fraction of Drum A is shaded? What fraction of Drum B? Do the two drums hold the same amount of fuel?","Explain why multiplying the top and the bottom of 3/4 by 2 does not change how much fuel that is.","Write two more fractions that are equal to 3/4."],key:["3/4 of Drum A and 6/8 of Drum B, and yes, they are the same amount. The drums are the same size and the shaded lengths match. Partial: both fractions right with no answer about the amounts is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Multiplying by 2 on the top and the bottom cuts every part in half, so there are twice as many parts and twice as many of them shaded. The pieces are smaller and there are more of them, so the amount stays the same. Partial: naming the doubling with nothing said about the pieces being smaller is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)","Any two of 6/8, 9/12, 12/16, 15/20, 75/100. Partial: one equal fraction given is approaching. (4.NF.A.1)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Snow Gauge",short:"Decimals",parts:[{std:["4.NF.C.6","4.NF.C.7"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Look at the line. Answer out loud. Write the last one on your paper.",desc:"writing fractions with denominators 10 and 100 as decimals, locating a decimal on a number line, and comparing two decimals to hundredths",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">The snow gauge measures the depth of new snow in parts of a meter.</p>`,fig:()=>F.numberLine({min:0,max:1,step:.1,labelEvery:.5,width:640}),qs:["Monday's gauge read 62/100 of a meter. Write that as a decimal. Point to about where it would sit on the line.","Tuesday's gauge read 0.6 of a meter. Write that as a fraction with 10 on the bottom, and again with 100 on the bottom.","Which day had more new snow, a reading of 0.6 or a reading of 0.57? Write it with the > or < sign and explain how you know."],key:["0.62, and it sits just past the sixth tick after 0, a little short of 0.7. Partial: 0.62 right with no place found on the line is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","6/10 and 60/100. Partial: one of the two fractions given is approaching. (4.NF.C.6)","0.6 > 0.57. Writing 0.6 as 0.60 makes it 60 hundredths against 57 hundredths, so 0.6 is the larger reading. Both readings measure parts of the same whole meter, so the comparison is a fair one. Partial: the right choice with the sign the wrong way round is approaching. (4.NF.C.7)"]}]},{id:21,title:"Units on the Trail",short:"Units",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.1"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Answer out loud. Make the table on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"knowing relative sizes of units and expressing a larger unit in a smaller one, including a conversion table",html:()=>`<p class="big" style="margin-top:0">Flags stand every 100 meters along the route, and the weather station is at flag twenty-two.</p>`,qs:["How far is the weather station from camp in meters? Give that distance in centimeters too.","A ration crate weighs 5 kilograms. How many grams is that?","On your paper make a two-column table for hours and minutes, with a row for 1 hour, 2 hours and 3 hours."],key:["2,200 meters, because 22 flags at 100 meters each is 22 × 100. In centimeters that is 220,000, because 1 meter is 100 centimeters. Partial: the meters right with no centimeters given is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)","5,000 grams, because 1 kilogram is 1,000 grams. Partial: 500 uses hundreds instead of thousands and does not count. (4.MD.A.1)","A two-column table with the pairs (1, 60), (2, 120) and (3, 180). The hours column on the left and the minutes column on the right, each row a matching pair. Partial: two of the three rows right is approaching. (4.MD.A.1)"]}]},{id:22,title:"Tent Floors",short:"Area",parts:[{std:["4.MD.A.3"],n:3,dur:55,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"applying the area and perimeter formulas for rectangles, including finding an unknown side",fig:()=>F.row([F.rect(12,7,"m"),"The big tent floor"]),qs:["What is the area of this floor? What is its perimeter?","A second tent floor has an area of 96 square meters, and one side is 8 meters. How long is the other side?","A storage floor has a perimeter of 30 meters, and one side is 9 meters. How long is the other side?"],key:["Area 84 square meters (12 × 7). Perimeter 38 meters (12 + 7 + 12 + 7, or 2 × 19). Partial: one of the area and the perimeter right is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","12 meters. Area is length times width, so 8 × w = 96 and w = 96 ÷ 8. Partial: 12 with no unit named is approaching. (4.MD.A.3)","6 meters. The two long sides and the two short sides add to 30, so 9 + w = 15 and w = 6. Or: half the perimeter is 15, and 15 − 9 = 6. (4.MD.A.3)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Sort the Shapes",short:"Shapes",parts:[{std:["4.G.A.2"],n:3,dur:50,dir:"Look at the four shapes. Answer out loud.",desc:"classifying two-dimensional figures by parallel sides, perpendicular sides, and the presence of a right angle",fig:()=>shapeSortFig(),qs:["Which shape is a right triangle? How do you know?","Which shapes have at least one pair of parallel sides? Which one has two pairs?","Shape A has two perpendicular sides. Which two are they, and what makes them perpendicular?"],key:["Shape A. It is a triangle and it has one right angle, marked by the small square at its bottom left corner. Shape D is a triangle with no right angle. Partial: naming Shape A with no reason is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","B and C both have a pair of parallel sides. B has two pairs: the top and bottom are parallel, and the two slanted sides are parallel to each other. C has only the top and bottom. Partial: naming the shapes with no account of which one has two pairs is approaching. (4.G.A.2)","The upright side and the bottom side, the two that meet at the marked corner. They are perpendicular because they meet at a right angle, a square corner. Partial: naming the two sides with no meaning for perpendicular given is approaching. (4.G.A.2)"]}]},{id:24,title:"Moving Camp",short:"Story",parts:[{wk:"W.4.3",std:["W.4.3"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:75,dir:WRITE_DIR,desc:"a short narrative with an orientation, ordered events, description or dialogue, and a conclusion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write the story of the day the team packed up base camp and moved it to a new spot on the ice. You are on the team. Write 5 or 6 sentences.</p>${bullets("Start by telling who is there and where it happens","Tell the events in the order they happen, with words like first, after that, by then","Use description or something someone says to bring one moment to life","End the story")}`,key:["Orients the reader: who is in the story and where and when it takes place (W.4.3)","Events unfold in a natural order, with transitional words such as first, after that, by then, finally (W.4.3)","Uses descriptive details or dialogue to develop at least one event (W.4.3)","Provides a conclusion that follows from the events (W.4.3)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Built for the Cold",short:"Structures",parts:[{std:["4-LS1-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two animals. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"arguing that named animal structures do a job that supports survival, growth or reproduction",html:()=>`<p class="big">A seal rests on the ice near the team's camp. A penguin stands further along the shore.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("seal",104)}${F.icon("penguin",104)}</div>
     <p class="big">The seal has a thick layer of fat called blubber under its skin, flippers instead of legs, and large lungs inside its body. The penguin has tightly packed feathers, a layer of fat, and wings shaped like paddles.</p>`,qs:["Name the job of the seal's blubber and the job of its flippers.","Build an argument that the penguin's wings suit the life it leads, even though it cannot fly.","Large lungs are an inside structure. How do they support the way a seal feeds?"],key:["Blubber holds body heat in, so the seal loses less warmth to the cold water, and it stores energy. Flippers push against water so the seal can swim and steer. (4-LS1-1)","A penguin finds its food in the water, so stiff paddle shaped wings that push against water let it swim fast enough to catch fish. Wings for flying would be large and light and would not work underwater. Look for the claim plus the reasoning about where it feeds. (4-LS1-1)","A seal feeds by diving. Large lungs hold a big store of air, so it can stay under long enough to find and catch food and still get back to the surface. (4-LS1-1)"]},{std:["4-LS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read what happens, then answer. You may draw your model.",desc:"modelling the path from sense organ to brain to response in an animal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A seal swims under the ice in water too dark to see through. Its long whiskers brush the swirls of water left behind a fish. The seal turns, follows the trail, and catches up with the fish.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("seal",104)}${F.icon("fish",104)}</div>`,qs:["Which sense took in the information, and which body part took it in?","The seal turned the right way. What had its brain worked out from the whisker signals?","Build a three step model of the path, from the swirls in the water to the seal turning."],key:["Touch, taken in by the whiskers. Accept a student who calls it feeling the water move. (4-LS1-2)","Which direction the fish had gone, and how recently. The whiskers only collect the signal, and the brain is where the direction is worked out and the turn is chosen. (4-LS1-2)","The whiskers take in the movement of the water, the brain processes it and chooses a direction, the body and flippers carry out the turn. No brain step is approaching. (4-LS1-2)"]}]},{id:26,title:"The Bare Rock Ridge",short:"Rock Layers",parts:[{std:["4-ESS1-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the ridge the team surveyed. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"reading rock layers and the fossils in them as evidence of landscape change",html:()=>`<p class="big">A bare rock ridge stands above the ice. The team recorded four layers in its side. Layers stack with the newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What the rock is like</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>top</td><td>broken rubble with no layering</td><td>none</td></tr>
     <tr><td>second</td><td>coal seam</td><td>fern and tree trunk prints</td></tr>
     <tr><td>third</td><td>pale sandstone</td><td>river snails</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bottom</td><td>dark fine mudstone</td><td>shellfish and sea lilies</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which layer formed first, and how do you know from the chart?","The second layer is coal with fern and tree prints in it. What kind of place was this when that layer formed?","Use all four layers to explain how this landscape changed over time."],key:["The bottom mudstone, because layers build upward and everything above it landed later. (4-ESS1-1)","A warm wet place with thick forest and swamp. Coal forms from plants that piled up and were buried, and ferns and tree trunks grew on land. (4-ESS1-1)","A sea floor with shellfish, then a river laying down sand, then a forested swamp that became coal, then rubble broken off and left on top. Any account reading the chart upward and naming the change from sea to land to forest counts, and noticing that a place now under ice was once a warm forest is the strongest answer. (4-ESS1-1)"]},{std:["4-ESS2-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the measurements. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using measurements to compare the rate of erosion by water, ice, wind or vegetation",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team set out four identical soft rock blocks for thirty days and weighed what was lost from each.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Block</th><th>What acted on it</th><th>Weight lost</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>kept dry and still inside a crate</td><td>1 gram</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>wind carrying ice crystals across it</td><td>46 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>water soaked in, frozen each night and thawed each day</td><td>88 grams</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>meltwater running steadily over it</td><td>22 grams</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Rank the three outdoor blocks from the most worn to the least worn, using the numbers.","Why does freezing and thawing break rock so quickly? Use the chart and what you know about ice.","What job does block 1 do in this investigation?"],key:["Block 3 at 88 grams, then block 2 at 46 grams, then block 4 at 22 grams. (4-ESS2-1)","Water seeps into small cracks, freezes, and expands as it turns to ice, pushing the crack wider. Thawing lets water seep deeper, and the cycle repeats every day and night, so block 3 lost the most of all. (4-ESS2-1)","It is the comparison block. It shows how little the rock loses when the weather is kept off it, so the other numbers can be read against it. Without it there is no way to know how much of the loss came from the weather. (4-ESS2-1)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Ice and Sea Floor on the Map",short:"Map Patterns",parts:[{std:["4-ESS2-2"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the survey chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"interpreting map data to describe patterns in Earth's features",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team drilled through the ice at six points along a straight line running inland from the shore, and measured the ice thickness and the height of the rock underneath.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("snowflake",80)}${F.icon("mountain",80)}${F.icon("wave",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Point</th><th>Distance inland</th><th>Ice thickness</th><th>Height of the rock below</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>0 km</td><td>60 m</td><td>at sea level</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>20 km</td><td>240 m</td><td>90 m above sea level</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>40 km</td><td>610 m</td><td>300 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>60 km</td><td>980 m</td><td>520 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>5</td><td>80 km</td><td>1,450 m</td><td>740 m</td></tr>
     <tr><td>6</td><td>100 km</td><td>1,900 m</td><td>910 m</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Describe the pattern in the ice thickness as the team moved inland.","Describe the pattern in the rock height, and say how the two patterns go together.","A seventh point is drilled at 120 km inland. Predict both measurements and say how you worked them out."],key:["It gets thicker the further inland they went, from 60 meters at the shore to 1,900 meters at 100 kilometers in. (4-ESS2-2)","The rock rises inland too, from sea level to 910 meters. Both rise together: the land climbs and the ice on top of it gets deeper, so the surface of the ice sheet climbs much faster than the rock does. (4-ESS2-2)","Both larger again, roughly 2,200 to 2,400 meters of ice over rock around 1,000 to 1,100 meters, because each 20 kilometers inland has added a few hundred meters of ice and roughly 200 meters of rock height. The reasoning from the pattern is what is being checked. (4-ESS2-2)"]},{std:["4-ESS3-2"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the two plans. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions that reduce the impact of a natural Earth process",html:()=>`<p class="big">Blowing snow drifts against the camp huts and buries the doors overnight. The team can build one thing with what it has.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Plan</th><th>What it does</th><th>Cost and time</th><th>Drawback</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: a snow fence set upwind of the huts</td><td>slows the wind so snow drops in a pile before it reaches the doors</td><td>low, half a day</td><td>the drift behind the fence must be moved once a season</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: raise the huts on legs above the drift line</td><td>wind blows underneath instead of piling against a wall</td><td>high, several days and a crane</td><td>stairs to climb, colder floors</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name a third way of your own to keep the doors clear.","Compare plan A and plan B. Which would you choose. What in the chart supports it?","Name a measure other than cost that you would compare these two plans on. Then say which plan wins on that measure."],key:["Any workable idea: turn the doors away from the wind, build a short entry tunnel or porch, shape the hut so wind flows around it, put the doors on the sheltered side, keep a shovel inside and clear the door each morning, set the huts end on to the wind. (4-ESS3-2)","Either plan, judged on the chart. Plan A is cheap and quick but leaves a drift to move each season. Plan B solves it for good but costs days of work and a crane and makes the huts colder underfoot. The reason read from the chart is what is rated. (4-ESS3-2)","Any measure the chart or the problem supports, with a plan matched to it: how much work it leaves each season, where B wins because the fence leaves a drift to move; how warm and easy the huts stay, where A wins because B gives colder floors and stairs to climb; how soon it is finished, where A wins at half a day against several days and a crane; how completely the doors stay clear, where B wins because the wind goes underneath. Or: how well it would stand up to a worse winter counts as well. Partial: a measure named with no plan matched to it, or cost given again under another name, is approaching. (4-ESS3-2)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Power at Base Camp",short:"Energy Sources",parts:[{std:["4-ESS3-1"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing where energy and fuels come from and how their use affects the environment",html:()=>`<p class="big">Base camp runs on four sources of energy.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Source</th><th>Natural resource behind it</th><th>Runs out?</th><th>One effect on the environment</th></tr>
     <tr><td>diesel generator</td><td>oil pumped from underground, flown in by plane</td><td>yes</td><td>exhaust, and a spill risk every time fuel is moved</td></tr>
     <tr><td>stove fuel canisters</td><td>gas from underground</td><td>yes</td><td>empty canisters must be carried out again</td></tr>
     <tr><td>wind turbine on the ridge</td><td>moving air</td><td>no</td><td>noise, and it must be built and shipped in</td></tr>
     <tr><td>solar panels</td><td>sunlight</td><td>no</td><td>useless during the long dark season</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Trace two of these sources back to the natural resource they come from.","The team wants to cut its fuel flights. Which two sources could replace some flights, and what limit does each one have?","Name one effect that gets smaller if the team uses the wind turbine instead of the generator. One new effect that appears."],key:["Any two read correctly: diesel from oil underground, canisters from gas underground, the turbine from moving air, the panels from sunlight. (4-ESS3-1)","The wind turbine and the solar panels. The turbine is noisy and had to be shipped in and built, and it only works when the wind blows. The panels give nothing at all during the dark season. (4-ESS3-1)","Exhaust and the spill risk from flying fuel in both get smaller. New effects: the turbine makes noise, it had to be manufactured and shipped, and on a still day the camp has no power from it. (4-ESS3-1)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Sledges on the Ice",short:"Speed",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the results. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using evidence to explain how the speed of an object relates to its energy",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team ran the same loaded sledge down an ice slope three times and let it run into a marker post set in soft snow. They measured how far the post was shifted.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Run</th><th>Speed at the bottom</th><th>Post shifted</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>2 meters each second</td><td>5 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>4 meters each second</td><td>19 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>6 meters each second</td><td>44 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["What happens to the distance the post is shifted as the speed goes up?","Which run carried the most energy? Name the measurement that is your evidence.","The speed doubled from run 1 to run 2. Did the post shift exactly twice as far? What does that tell you about speed and energy?"],key:["It gets larger, and by bigger steps each time: 5 cm, 19 cm, 44 cm. (4-PS3-1)","Run 3, at 6 meters each second, because it shifted the post 44 cm, farther than either other run. (4-PS3-1)","No. It shifted nearly four times as far, from 5 cm to 19 cm. Doubling the speed gives much more than double the energy. Noticing that the growth is uneven is the heart of this item. (4-PS3-1)"]},{std:["4-PS3-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. A prediction needs a reason, not the right answer.",desc:"asking a question and predicting the energy change in a collision",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two sledges of the same size are on smooth ice. One carries a crate of tinned food and is sliding along. The other is empty and standing still in its path.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sled",96)}${F.icon("box",96)}</div>`,qs:["Ask one question about this collision that a test could answer.","Predict what happens to each sledge after they meet.","The same test is run on rough snow instead of smooth ice. Predict what is different, and say why."],key:["Any testable question: how far does the empty sledge slide, does the loaded one stop completely, does the knock sound louder at a higher speed, what happens if both are loaded. (4-PS3-3)","The empty sledge is pushed forward and slides away. The loaded sledge slows down and may stop. Both halves make the full prediction. (4-PS3-3)","The empty sledge travels a shorter distance on rough snow, because friction takes energy out of it as heat and scraping. Energy going somewhere other than motion is the idea to listen for. (4-PS3-3)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Energy Around the Camp",short:"Transfer",parts:[{std:["4-PS3-2"],n:4,dur:75,dir:"For each observation, say how the energy traveled. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"matching observations to energy transferred by sound, light, heat or electric current",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four things noticed at base camp in one day.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th></th><th>What was noticed</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>A cable runs from the wind turbine to the hut, and the heater inside comes on.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>A mug of soup is set on the metal table and the table top under it becomes warm.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>The camp horn is sounded and a team two kilometers out on the ice hears it.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>A flashlight is switched on in the tent and the far wall of the tent lights up.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["How did the energy travel in observation 1?","How did it travel in observation 2?","How did it travel in observation 3?","How did it travel in observation 4?"],key:["By an electric current, through the cable from the turbine to the heater, where it becomes heat. (4-PS3-2)","By heat, from the hot soup through the mug into the metal and out across the table top. (4-PS3-2)","By sound, through the air across two kilometers of ice to their ears. (4-PS3-2)","By light, from the flashlight across the tent to the wall. (4-PS3-2)"]},{std:["4-PS3-4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Draw your device and label it. Then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"designing, testing and improving a device that converts energy from one form to another",html:()=>`<p class="big">The radio battery is flat and the generator is being saved for the drill. Design a device that turns one kind of energy into electrical energy. It has to charge the radio. You may use a hand crank, a small generator, or a propeller. You may also use wire or a weight on a rope. A panel that takes in daylight will do too.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("radio",96)}${F.icon("wind",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw and label your device. Show the energy going in and the electrical energy coming out.","Name the kind of energy going in and the kind coming out.","You test it and the radio charges very slowly. Name one change you would make and say why it should help."],key:["Any labeled drawing with a path: a propeller in the wind turning a small generator wired to the radio; a crank and generator; a falling weight on a rope turning the generator; a daylight panel wired in. Labels and the path through it make it a design. (4-PS3-4)","Going in: motion of the wind, the crank or the falling weight, or light from the sun. Coming out: electrical energy, plus some heat and sound. Both forms named. (4-PS3-4)","Any change with a reason: a larger propeller to catch more wind, mount it higher where the wind is stronger, gear the generator to spin faster, a heavier weight or a longer drop, shorter and thicker wire so less energy is lost as heat. (4-PS3-4)"]}]},{id:31,title:"Sound in the Ice, Light on the Snow",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["4-PS4-1"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Look at the two drawings. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"using a wave model to describe amplitude and wavelength and the effect waves have on objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team tapped the ice with a hammer and drew the wave the instruments recorded. They did it once with a light tap and once with a hard strike.</p>
     <div class="figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 520 300" width="520" role="img" aria-label="Two waves drawn above one another with letters A and B marking parts of the first wave">
       <text x="10" y="18" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">light tap</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="70" x2="500" y2="70" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 70 q30 -26 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
       <line x1="50" y1="70" x2="50" y2="57" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="62" y="63" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">A</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="112" x2="140" y2="112" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="4"/>
       <text x="80" y="134" font-size="22" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif" text-anchor="middle">B</text>
       <text x="10" y="186" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="Andika,sans-serif">hard strike</text>
       <line x1="20" y1="240" x2="500" y2="240" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 6"/>
       <path d="M20 240 q30 -82 60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0 t60 0" fill="none" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="5"/>
     </svg></div>`,qs:["Which letter marks the wavelength and which marks the amplitude?","Which measurement changed between the two strikes, and what would a listener notice?","A loose pebble is sitting on the ice. It hops when the hard strike is made. It does not hop when the tap is made. Explain that with the drawings."],key:["B marks the wavelength, the distance from one crest to the next. A marks the amplitude, the height from the flat line to the crest. (4-PS4-1)","The amplitude grew. In a sound wave a larger amplitude is heard as a louder sound. The wavelength did not change, so the pitch is the same. (4-PS4-1)","A larger amplitude carries more energy, and a wave with enough energy can make an object move. The hard strike's wave carries enough to lift the pebble; the tap's wave does not. (4-PS4-1)"]},{std:["4-PS4-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing. You may draw arrows.",desc:"modelling how light reflects from objects into the eye so the objects can be seen",html:()=>`<p class="big">Outside, the snow is so bright that the team wears dark goggles. Inside the equipment crate with the lid shut it is completely dark. A red flag folded in there cannot be seen.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("sunGlasses",96)}${F.icon("flag",96)}</div>`,qs:["Draw or tell the path of the light when a team member sees the snow. Name every place it goes.","Dark goggles make everything look dimmer. Explain how they do that, using the path of the light.","Why can the folded flag inside the closed crate not be seen at all?"],key:["Light leaves the sun, travels to the snow, reflects off the snow, and travels into the eye. All three legs are needed. (4-PS4-2)","The goggles sit in the path between the snow and the eye and let only part of the light through, so less light reaches the eye and everything looks dimmer. (4-PS4-2)","No light gets into the closed crate, so there is none to reflect off the flag and reach an eye. An object must send light to the eye to be seen. (4-PS4-2)"]},{std:["4-PS4-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Read the two systems. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"comparing two solutions that use patterns to send information",html:()=>`<p class="big">A field party out on the ice and base camp need three messages: work going well, send the sledge, and returning now.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>System</th><th>How a message is sent</th><th>Needs</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Radio beeps</td><td>one beep, two beeps, or three beeps on an agreed channel</td><td>charged batteries at both ends; reaches many kilometers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Marker flags on a pole</td><td>one flag, two flags, or a flag flown upside down</td><td>clear air and a short distance; no power needed</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Say what the pattern is in each system.","The field party is 20 kilometers out and its radio batteries are flat. What is the trouble with each system now?","Design a third system of your own for the same three messages, using neither radio nor flags."],key:["In the radio system the pattern is the number of beeps. In the flag system it is the number of flags and the way a flag is flown. An agreed pattern standing for a message is what carries the information. (4-PS4-3)","The radio cannot be used with flat batteries. Flags cannot be seen at 20 kilometers, and need clear air. Neither system works at that distance without power, which is why real parties carry more than one method. (4-PS4-3)","Any pattern with three agreed signals: three patterns of lamp flashes, a mirror flashed once, twice or three times at the sun, three agreed shapes stamped in the snow for a plane to read, three patterns of taps sent along a rope or the ice, three positions of a marker on a line. (4-PS4-3)"]}]},{id:32,title:"A Door the Snow Does Not Block",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["3-5-ETS1-1"],n:3,dur:60,dir:"Read the situation, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria for success and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">The store hut door opens outward and snow drifts against it every night. So two people have to dig it out each morning. The team has one afternoon, packing crates, spare canvas, rope, and no new materials coming until spring.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("box",88)}${F.icon("snowCloud",88)}</div>`,qs:["State the problem in one sentence, in your own words.","Write two criteria that would tell the team the fix works.","Write two constraints from the situation."],key:["Something like: the store hut needs a way in each morning without digging out a drift. A problem statement, not a solution. (3-5-ETS1-1)","Any two testable criteria: the door can be opened in the morning without digging, one person can do it, the opening is wide enough to carry a crate through, nothing new has to be bought. (3-5-ETS1-1)","One afternoon, only crates, canvas and rope, no new materials until spring. Any two. Giving a criterion here instead is approaching. (3-5-ETS1-1)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-2"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Study the three designs. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"generating and comparing solutions against the criteria and constraints",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three fixes for the door.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Fix</th><th>What it is</th><th>Materials</th><th>Build time</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>rehang the door so it opens inward</td><td>tools only</td><td>short</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>build a canvas and crate porch that faces away from the wind</td><td>crates, canvas, rope</td><td>most of the afternoon</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>fit a heated mat outside the door to melt the drift</td><td>a mat and cable to be flown in</td><td>waits until spring</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which fix breaks a constraint? Name it.","Compare fix 1 and fix 2 against the criteria. Which do you expect to work better, and why?","Add a fourth idea of your own that fits the materials and the time."],key:["Fix 3. The mat and cable have to be flown in and nothing arrives until spring, so it cannot be built this afternoon. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Either, argued from the criteria. Fix 1 is quick and means the drift no longer blocks the door opening, though snow still piles in the doorway and falls in. Fix 2 keeps the drift from forming at the entrance at all but takes the whole afternoon and could itself collect snow. (3-5-ETS1-2)","Any idea in the materials on hand: stack crates as a wind break upwind of the door, hang canvas as a flap over the doorway, cut a second door on the sheltered wall, build a short tunnel of crates out from the door, clear a shallow trench the wind will fill instead. (3-5-ETS1-2)"]},{std:["3-5-ETS1-3"],n:3,dur:75,dir:"Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"planning a fair test with controlled variables and naming a failure point",html:()=>`<p class="big">The team wants to find out whether rehanging the door or building the porch keeps the entrance clearer. There are two identical huts side by side.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("tent",96)}${F.icon("snowflake",96)}</div>`,qs:["Plan the test. What will you measure?","Name two things you must keep the same for both huts so the test is fair.","Name one failure point: where your fix is most likely to give way first."],key:["Measure the depth of snow at each doorway each morning in centimeters, or the minutes of digging each one needs, over the same several nights. A measured result, not an impression. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any two: the same nights and the same weather, huts facing the same way, the same wind exposure, the same number of times each door is opened, the same person measuring the same way. (3-5-ETS1-3)","Any sensible weak spot: the rope lashings on the canvas work loose in wind, the porch itself collects a drift at its own mouth, the inward opening door lets a pile of snow fall inside, the canvas tears, crates shift and the wind gets behind them. (3-5-ETS1-3)"]}]},{id:33,title:"Four California Cities",short:"Regions",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.1"],n:4,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart of four California cities. Answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the physical and human geographic features that define places and regions in California",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four cities in California, with where they sit and what the weather records show.</p>
     <div class="figure">${F.icon("wave",80)}${F.icon("wheat",80)}${F.icon("cactus",80)}</div>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>City</th><th>Region</th><th>Height</th><th>Hottest month average</th><th>Rain in a year</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Eureka, far north coast</td><td>Coast</td><td>13 m</td><td>15 degrees Celsius</td><td>100 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Sacramento</td><td>Central Valley</td><td>8 m</td><td>25 degrees Celsius</td><td>46 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Bishop, east of the Sierra</td><td>Mountains and basin</td><td>1,250 m</td><td>25 degrees Celsius</td><td>13 cm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Needles, far south east</td><td>Desert</td><td>150 m</td><td>35 degrees Celsius</td><td>11 cm</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Which city is coolest in its hottest month, and which is hottest? Name their regions.","Eureka and Needles are both near sea level, yet their rainfall is very different. Explain why, using where each one sits.","Bishop is 1,250 meters up and still gets only 13 cm of rain. Explain how a place can be high and dry at the same time.","Name one physical feature and one human feature of any of these cities. Then say what makes each one that kind."],key:["Eureka is coolest, at 15 degrees in its hottest month, on the Coast. Needles is hottest, at 35 degrees, in the Desert. Partial: one of the two cities named with its region is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Eureka sits on the open north coast where ocean air and storms arrive first, so it gets 100 cm. Needles sits far inland behind mountains, so moist air has already dropped its rain before reaching it, leaving 11 cm. Partial: one of the two cities explained is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","It is east of the Sierra Nevada, in the shelter of the range. Air crossing the mountains drops its rain and snow on the western slope, so the eastern side stays dry however high it is. Partial: naming the mountains with no account of where the rain falls is approaching. (HSS-4.1)","Physical: the ocean, the valley floor, the mountains, the elevation, the rainfall. Human: the cities themselves, the port at Eureka, the capitol in Sacramento, roads and canals. Physical comes from nature, human is built by people. Partial: one of each named with no difference stated is approaching. (HSS-4.1)"]}]},{id:34,title:"Tools, Trade and Gold",short:"California Past",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.2"],n:3,dur:190,dir:"Read the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"describing the cultural and economic life and the interactions among the peoples of early California",html:()=>`<p class="big">What people built and used in three periods of early California.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period</th><th>What they built and used</th><th>How they got what they could not make</th></tr>
     <tr><td>First peoples, for thousands of years</td><td>plank canoes on the southern coast, tightly woven baskets that held water, stone mortars for grinding acorns, bows and fishing nets</td><td>trade routes between nations, with shell beads used as money along the coast</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Spanish mission period, 1769 onward</td><td>adobe brick buildings with tile roofs and bell towers, irrigation ditches, water wheels, workshops for leather, cloth and candles</td><td>supply ships, and carts along El Camino Real</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Mexican rancho period, 1820s onward</td><td>adobe rancho houses, corrals, saddles and branding tools</td><td>trading hides and tallow to ships from the eastern states and Europe</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Not everything a nation built was for daily use. Each nation had its own religious beliefs and its own ceremonies, and they differed from nation to nation. Miwok nations built a large round earth covered ceremonial house for their dances, and nothing else was done in it. Chumash ceremonies were led by trained specialists, and the shell beads that served as money on the coast were used in them as well.</p>
     <p class="big">Franciscan missionaries sent from Spain founded the missions and planned those buildings. Their purpose was to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops, herding and trades, and the building and farming work was done by Native Californians under the missionaries' direction.</p>`,qs:["Name two things the first peoples of California made. Then say what each one was for. Then use the note. Name one thing built or used for a ceremony rather than for daily work.","According to the note, what were the Franciscan missionaries sent to California to do? The missions also built irrigation ditches and water wheels. They needed something in order to farm here. What does that tell you?","Each period got what it could not make in a different way. Describe the three ways and say which reached the farthest."],key:["Any two with a use: plank canoes for fishing and travel on the ocean, watertight baskets for carrying and cooking, stone mortars for grinding acorns into meal, nets and bows for catching food. For the ceremony half: the large round earth covered ceremonial house Miwok nations built for their dances and used for nothing else, or the shell beads used in Chumash ceremonies as well as as money. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so two things with uses and nothing on the ceremony half sits there. (HSS-4.2)","The Franciscans were sent to convert Native Californians to Christianity and to teach them Spanish crops, herding and trades. On the ditches and wheels: summers here are long and dry, so crops needed water brought to them. Ditches carried water to the fields and wheels lifted or moved it, which means farming depended on moving water, not on summer rain. Both halves are needed for full credit. Partial: one half without the other is approaching, so naming the purpose with nothing on the ditches sits there. (HSS-4.2)","The first peoples traded nation to nation within California, the missions used supply ships and carts along El Camino Real, the ranchos traded across the ocean to the eastern states and Europe. The rancho trade reached farthest. Partial: the three ways described with no answer about which reached farthest is approaching. (HSS-4.2)"]},{std:["HSS-4.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the political and economic life of California from the Bear Flag Republic through the Gold Rush to statehood",html:()=>`<p class="big">How mining changed, and how California was governed, between 1846 and 1852.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1846</td><td>Settlers at Sonoma raised a bear flag and declared a republic. Weeks later the war between the United States and Mexico reached California.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1848</td><td>The war ended and California passed to the United States. Gold was found at Coloma on the American River.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1849</td><td>Miners panned and used rockers in the rivers. Delegates met at Monterey and wrote a state constitution.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1850</td><td>California was admitted as the 31st state. Mining camps had grown into towns with stores, courts and newspapers.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1853 onward</td><td>Companies used water cannons on hillsides, which took money and machines no single miner had.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Why did California need a constitution written before it could become a state?","Mining went from a person with a pan to a company with water cannons. What changed for an ordinary miner?","Name two groups of people whose lives the Gold Rush changed. Then say how each one was changed."],key:["A state needs a plan of government: who makes laws, who carries them out, who judges. Congress would not admit California without one, so delegates wrote it at Monterey in 1849. Partial: saying Congress required it with no account of what a constitution sets out is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","An ordinary miner could no longer compete. The easy river gold was taken and the methods that reached the rest needed money, water systems and machines, so most miners ended up working for wages, farming, or running a business instead. Partial: saying mining got harder with no account of the money and machinery needed is approaching. (HSS-4.3)","Any two with a reason: Native Californians lost land and food sources and their numbers fell sharply; miners from Mexico, Chile, China, Ireland and the eastern states arrived and most found little gold; merchants and shippers grew wealthy; Mexican Californian families often lost land under new claims and laws; farmers downstream suffered from mining debris in the rivers. Partial: two groups named with no account of how each was changed is approaching. (HSS-4.3)"]}]},{id:35,title:"Industry and the Three Branches",short:"Government",parts:[{std:["HSS-4.4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"tracing California's development into an agricultural and industrial power since the 1850s",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four California industries and when each one grew.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Industry</th><th>When it grew</th><th>Why California suited it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Irrigated farming</td><td>1870s onward</td><td>deep valley soil, long warm growing season, mountain snow stored and carried by canal</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Oil</td><td>1890s onward</td><td>large underground oil fields in the south and in the valley</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Aircraft and aerospace</td><td>1930s onward</td><td>clear flying weather most of the year, open land for runways and plants, ports and rail</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Computers and software</td><td>1960s onward</td><td>universities, skilled workers, and companies clustered near one another</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Pick one industry and explain how California's physical geography helped it grow.","Computers are the one industry in the chart that does not depend on soil, weather or what is underground. What did it depend on instead?","Name one effect these industries had on where people in California lived."],key:["Any one with the geography named: farming needed deep soil, a long warm season and mountain snow; oil needed underground fields; aircraft needed clear flying weather and open flat land. Partial: an industry named with no geography behind it is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","People and knowledge: universities, skilled workers, and many companies working near one another so ideas and workers moved between them. Partial: naming skilled workers with nothing said about companies clustering is still proficient; naming money alone is approaching. (HSS-4.4)","Any supported effect: farm towns grew along canals and railroads, oil brought work and growth to Los Angeles and Kern County, aircraft plants drew hundreds of thousands to southern California, computer work filled the valley south of San Francisco Bay. Partial: an effect asserted with no industry tied to it is approaching. (HSS-4.4)"]},{std:["HSS-4.5"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study the chart, then answer out loud or in writing.",desc:"explaining the structures, functions and powers of government and how the branches check one another",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four moments that show the branches checking one another.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Moment</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>The legislature passed a bill. The governor rejected it, so it did not become law.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>The legislature voted again, with a large enough majority in both houses, and the bill became law without the governor's signature.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>A court decided that part of a new law did not agree with the constitution, so that part could not be enforced.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>At the next election voters chose different representatives, and the new legislature changed the law.</td></tr></table>`,qs:["Name the branch acting in each of moments 1, 2 and 3.","Moments 1 and 2 go together. Explain what they show about how power is shared.","Moment 4 is not a branch of government. What is it, and why does it matter?"],key:["Moment 1 the executive, the governor. Moment 2 the legislature. Moment 3 the judicial branch, the courts. Partial: two of the three moments matched is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","The governor can block a bill, but the legislature can still pass it with a large enough majority. Neither one has the last word by itself, which is what sharing power means. Partial: describing the veto with nothing said about the legislature overriding it is approaching. (HSS-4.5)","It is the voters. Elections are how the people themselves check every branch: they can replace the people holding the offices, which is the power the public holds in the system. Partial: naming the voters with no account of why it matters is approaching. (HSS-4.5)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={2:{id:"L2",lv:true,title:"Level Up: One Tenth",std:["5.NBT.A.1"],needs:["4.NBT.A.1"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Explain how you know.",html:()=>`<p class="huge">4,400</p><ol class="qlist"><li>In 4,400, the 4 in the hundreds place is worth what part of the 4 in the thousands place?</li><li>A 6 in the tens place is worth 60. What is one tenth of that, and in which place would a 6 be worth it?</li></ol>`,key:["One tenth of it. The 4 in the hundreds place is 400 and the 4 in the thousands place is 4,000, and 400 is 1/10 of 4,000. (5.NBT.A.1)","6, in the ones place. 6 is 1/10 of 60. (5.NBT.A.1)"]},3:{id:"L3",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Exact Words",std:["RL.5.1"],needs:["RL.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Find the words in the story and read them out loud exactly.",html:()=>`${paras(SLED,"sm")}<ol class="qlist"><li>Read me the exact words from the story that show Tova was cold.</li><li>Read me the exact words Ravi said about what to do if she fell off.</li></ol>`,key:["Reads exact words, such as: because her hand was shaking. Also accept: to keep her mind off the cold. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)","Reads the exact words: If you fall off, let go and stay still. I will come back for you. (RL.5.1, quoting accurately)"]},7:{id:"L7",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Fuel Math",std:["5.NF.A.2"],needs:["4.NF.A.2"],n:2,dir:"Solve number 1 on your paper and hold it up. Answer number 2 out loud.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A fuel drum was 3/4 full. The stove burned 1/6 of a drum. How much of the drum is left?</li><li>A helper says 1/3 of a drum plus 2/5 of a drum is 3/8 of a drum. Without adding, explain how you know that cannot be right.</li></ol>`,key:["7/12 of the drum. 3/4 is 9/12 and 1/6 is 2/12, so 9/12 − 2/12 = 7/12. (5.NF.A.2)","A sum cannot be smaller than one of its parts, and 3/8 is less than 2/5. Benchmark check: 2/5 is almost 1/2 and 1/3 is more than 1/4, so the sum is well over 1/2, while 3/8 is under 1/2. (5.NF.A.2, using benchmarks to judge whether an answer is reasonable)"]},8:{id:"L8",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Three Texts",std:["RI.5.9"],needs:["RI.4.9"],n:2,dir:"Read the third text. Then answer out loud using all three texts.",html:()=>`<div class="texts"><article><h3>Text 1: ${PEN_A.title}</h3><p>${PEN_A.body}</p></article><article><h3>Text 2: ${PEN_B.title}</h3><p>${PEN_B.body}</p></article><article><h3>Text 3: ${PEN_C.title}</h3><p>${PEN_C.body}</p></article></div><ol class="qlist"><li>Use all three texts. Explain how the male penguin lives through the coldest months while he is holding the egg.</li><li>Which text would you use to find out how long the male goes without food, and which texts would you use to find out how he stays warm? Explain.</li></ol>`,key:["Text 3: he holds the egg on his feet under a flap of skin and eats nothing for about two months. Text 2: he starts with almost half his weight as fat, and his overlapping feathers and the warmed blood coming back from his feet hold heat in. Text 1: he stands in a huddle that is more than 20 degrees warmer inside and shuffles out of the wind, so he burns less of that stored fat. (RI.5.9)","Text 3 for the length of the fast, about two months. Texts 1 and 2 for staying warm: Text 2 gives feathers, fat, and blood flow, and Text 1 gives the huddle and the shuffle. (RI.5.9)"]},13:{id:"L13",lv:true,title:"Level Up: Paired Words",std:["L.5.1"],needs:["L.4.1"],n:2,dir:"Say each sentence out loud the correct way.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Finish it with either and or, or with neither and nor: ____ the radio ____ the spare radio worked in the storm. Nothing worked at all.</li><li>By the time the plane landed, the team ____ for three days. (wait) Start with had been.</li></ol>`,key:["Neither the radio nor the spare radio worked in the storm. (L.5.1, correlative conjunctions)","had been waiting (L.5.1, perfect progressive tense)"]}};const PASSAGE_STD=["RL.4.1","RI.4.1","RI.4.9","W.4.8","W.4.2","RL.5.1","RI.5.9"];const MULT_N=12,FRAC_DENS=[1,2,3,4,5,6,8,10,12];let multSel=[0,0],fracOn={},pvVal="";const STARTERS={facts:["At base camp, the team ____.","One rule is ____.","For example, ____.","Also, ____.","In conclusion, ____."],opinion:["The expedition should travel by ____ because ____.","One reason is ____.","For instance, ____.","In addition, ____.","That is why ____."],"W.4.1":["The team should ____ today.","One reason is ____.","That is why ____."],"W.4.8":["Source 1 says ____.","Source 2 says ____.","In my own words, ____."],"W.4.3":["The morning we moved camp, ____.","First, ____.","After that, ____.","“____,” said ____.","By the time ____, we ____."]};function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function pvTable(){const cols=["Hundred<br>thousands","Ten<br>thousands","Thousands","Hundreds","Tens","Ones"],ip=pvVal.replace(/[^0-9]/g,"");let h="<tr>";cols.forEach(c=>h+=`<th>${c}</th>`);h+="</tr><tr>";cols.forEach((c,i)=>{const k=ip.length-(cols.length-i);h+=`<td>${k>=0?ip[k]:""}</td>`});return h+"</tr>"}function pvHtml(){return`<label class="pv-in">Type a number<input type="text" inputmode="numeric" id="pvInput" value="${pvVal}" autocomplete="off"></label><div class="tool-scroll"><table class="pv" id="pvTable">${pvTable()}</table></div><p class="small">Each digit lands in its place. The place to the left is always worth 10 times as much.</p>`}function fracHtml(){return FRAC_DENS.map(d=>{const on=fracOn[d]||0;let r=`<div class="fs-row">`;for(let k=0;k<d;k++)r+=`<button class="fs-part${d>=10?" sm":""}${k<on?" on":""}" data-d="${d}" data-k="${k}">${d===1?"1 whole":"1/"+d}</button>`;return r+"</div>"}).join("")+`<p class="small">Click a piece to shade up to it. Click the last shaded piece to clear the row.</p>`}const SENT_ALIAS={opinion:"W.4.1",facts:"W.4.2",story:"W.4.3","W.4.1":"opinion","W.4.2":"facts","W.4.3":"story"};function sentKey(st){const ps=st&&st.parts&&st.parts.length?st.parts:st?[st]:[];const pick=c=>!c?null:STARTERS[c]?c:SENT_ALIAS[c]&&STARTERS[SENT_ALIAS[c]]?SENT_ALIAS[c]:null;for(const p of ps){const a=pick(p.alt&&p.alt.key||p.wk||p.wt);if(a)return a;for(const c of p.std||[]){const k=pick(c);if(k)return k}}return null}function sentHtml(st){const k=sentKey(st);return`<ul class="starters">${(STARTERS[k]||STARTERS["W.4.1"]).map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}const TOOLS={mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:["4.OA.A.1","4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3","5.NF.A.2"],html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const rr=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===rr&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[rr,k];r()})},pv:{name:"Place value chart",std:["4.NBT.A.1","4.NBT.A.3","5.NBT.A.1"],html:pvHtml,bind:b=>{const inp=b.querySelector("#pvInput"),tb=b.querySelector("#pvTable");if(inp&&tb)inp.oninput=()=>{pvVal=inp.value.replace(/[^0-9]/g,"");tb.innerHTML=pvTable()}}},frac:{name:"Fraction strips",std:["4.NF.A.1","4.NF.A.2","4.NF.C.6","5.NF.A.2"],html:fracHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll(".fs-part").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const d=+x.dataset.d,k=+x.dataset.k;fracOn[d]=fracOn[d]===k+1?0:k+1;r()})},sent:{name:"Sentence starters",std:["W.4.1","W.4.2","W.4.3","W.4.8"],html:sentHtml}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.1"],msg:"The place value chart names the places and lines up the digits, so the student does not have to recall place names. The ten times comparisons still need the student's own reasoning."},{t:"pv",std:["4.NBT.A.3"],msg:"With the chart the student can see which digit decides the rounding. Note it if the chart did the place finding."},{t:"frac",msg:"Fraction strips can show the answer. Rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show."},{t:"mult",std:["4.NF.A.2"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the common denominators. This stop still shows whether the student can pick a common denominator and compare."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence starters supply the opening, the linking words, and the closing, so count those checks as supported, not independent."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the passage was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"mult",std:["4.OA.A.1"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the product, so rate on whether the student can say the comparison in words and write the equation, not on the fact."},{t:"mult",std:["4.MD.A.1","4.MD.A.3"],msg:"The multiplication chart supplies the products, so rate the conversions and the area and perimeter work on the reasoning, not the arithmetic."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.A.1"],msg:"Fraction strips line the equal parts up side by side, which shows the equivalence directly. Rate on whether the student can explain what the strips show."},{t:"frac",std:["4.NF.C.6"],msg:"The strips go only to twelfths, so they help with tenths but not hundredths. Note which readings the student worked out with them."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,passageStd:PASSAGE_STD}}});
